 61   c  ABST$The evaluated spectroscopic data are presented for known nuclides 
 61  2c  of mass 61 (Sc,Ti,V,Cr,Mn,Fe,Co,Ni,Cu,Zn,Ga,Ge). Excited-state data    
 61  2c  are nonexistent for {+61}Sc, {+61}Ti, {+61}V, and {+61}Ge. Significant 
 61  3c  amounts of new data have been added since the previous Nuclear Data    
 61  4c  Sheets evaluation of A=61 nuclides (1999Bh04). {+61}Sc nuclide is now  
 61  5c  experimentally known, but without any knowledge of its half-life.  The 
 61  6c  ground state half-lives of {+61}Ti and {+61}V are now determined.      
 61  8c  Excited-state data have become available for {+61}Cr, {+61}Mn and      
 61  9c  {+61}Ga, while for {+61}Ge, five excited states were erroneously       
 61  Ac  assigned in the previous evaluation, these have been omitted here.     
 61  Bc  Improved and extensive high-spin excitations are available for         
 61  Cc  {+61}Fe, {+61}Cu and {+61}Zn, including several new superdeformed      
 61  Dc  structures in {+61}Cu and {+61}Zn. Thermal neutron capture |g-ray data 
 61  Ec  are available in detail from 2004Ra23. The radioactive decay schemes   
 61  Fc  of {+61}Sc and {+61}Ti are not known, while those for {+61}V, {+61}Fe  
 61  Gc  and {+61}Ge are poorly known. This work benefited from the earlier     
 61  Hc  full evaluations of A=61 published by 1999Bh04, 1983Ek01 and 1975Au05  
 61  Ic  and the one published in an 'update' mode by 1992Zh31. The data and    
 61  Jc  conclusions presented in the current work supersede those in all the   
 61  Kc  previous evaluations.                                                  
 61   c  CUT$Literature up to Jan 25, 2015 has been consulted. The main source  
 61  2c  used for bibliographic information is the NSR database (2011Pr03) at   
 61  3c  www.nndc.bnl.gov/nsr/.                                                 
